⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535879 true] [17592186167476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535879 true] [17592186167476 1100 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139535879 true] [17592186167476 1100 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}}⟨t, 2, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139535879 false] [17592186167476 1145 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372008 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1089 "q-127613" 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1100 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1113 #uuid "126d7092-f259-50a6-8d9e-e771713a98c4" 13194139535879 true] [17592186372007 1145 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{t} & 0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0-0,-(t+(3-t))